          <p>
            The Vornado math is a useful example. The Evap40 runs two wicks, Vornado sells them as a two-pack at $14.99, and it says to replace them every four to eight weeks depending on use. Over a five-month heating season that is roughly three to five sets, or about $45 to $75 a season at list price. Third-party wicks cost less, but fit and quality vary, so check reviews and dimensions before you switch.
          </p>

          <p>
            Honeywell's Filter C guidance for the HEV320 is 30 to 60 days, with the warning that hard water may mean changing it sooner. Honeywell also lists the signs it is time: the wick feels hard and crusty, it gives off an odor, or moisture output drops. Those signs apply to every wick on this list and are a better trigger than the calendar if your water is very soft or very hard.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two of our picks change the equation. Levoit's Superior Studio Smart uses a washable wick that Levoit says lasts up to two years if you wash it weekly, which turns a monthly purchase into a weekly chore. The Venta AH530 has no wick at all; it uses a stack of plastic discs you rinse every 10 to 14 days. Its running cost is the Venta water treatment additive the maker recommends, plus a much higher purchase price.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whatever you buy, order wicks before the season starts. Replacement wicks for popular models sell out in the first cold snap, and a humidifier running on a wick that should have been changed a month ago is doing a fraction of its job. Buying a season's worth in October is the simplest way to keep the running cost predictable.
          </p>
