        <div id="noise" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Noise: You Are Hearing a Fan
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every evaporative humidifier makes noise for the same reason: a fan has to move air across the wick. Unlike an ultrasonic unit, which is close to silent apart from a faint gurgle, an evaporative model sounds like a small fan, because that is what it is. On low it is usually a gentle hum; on high it can be distracting in a quiet bedroom.
          </p>

          <p>
            Only some makers publish noise figures, and they are measured in different ways, so compare them with care. Levoit lists the Superior Studio at 19 to 45 decibels measured 1.5 meters away and the Superior 6000S at 28 to 45 decibels. Abt's listing for the Venta AH530 gives 24, 34 and 44 decibels across its three speeds. Vornado, Honeywell and AIRCARE do not give decibel figures in the listings we checked.
          </p>

          <p>
            In practice the useful question is whether the unit has a quiet low or sleep setting that still holds the room in range. A large unit running on low is often quieter than a small one running on high, which is another argument for sizing up. Vornado notes that the Evap40's low speed is meant for quieter running in small rooms and high for faster humidification in large ones.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some people find a steady fan sound helps them sleep and some do not. If you are a light sleeper who needs silence, an evaporative humidifier may never suit you, and an ultrasonic model on distilled water is the better choice. Our picks for the <a href="/best-humidifier-for-bedroom/">best humidifier for a bedroom</a> weigh noise above everything else.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="water" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Tap Water, Hard Water and What the Wick Catches
          </h2>

          <p>
            One of the main reasons to buy an evaporative humidifier is that you can use tap water. The minerals stay in the wick, so there is no white dust, and you do not need to buy distilled water. Venta's retailer listing for the AH530 says tap water is safe to use, and Levoit says the Superior Studio's wick removes impurities from tap water.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hard water still has a cost; it just shows up in the wick rather than on your furniture. The harder your water, the faster scale builds in the wick and the sooner it stops wicking properly. If your wicks are crusty and stiff after three weeks, your water is hard and you are better off budgeting for more wicks than trying to stretch them.
          </p>

          <p>
            Distilled water is not required, but it extends wick life and reduces scale in the tank and base. Some owners in very hard-water areas use a mix, filling with filtered or distilled water part of the time. Whatever you use, empty and refill with fresh water rather than topping up a tank that has been sitting for days.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not add anything to the water unless the maker says to. Essential oils, vapor rubs and household chemicals can damage wicks and plastics, and Venta's own additive is designed specifically for its disc system. If you want a scent, use a model with a built-in aroma feature, such as the aromatherapy options Abt lists for the Venta AH530, rather than pouring oil into the tank.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cleaning" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Cleaning and Mold
          </h2>

          <p>
            Evaporative humidifiers are less likely than ultrasonic ones to put bacteria into the air as droplets, but the tank, base and wick are still damp, warm and full of organic matter, which is exactly where mold and bacteria grow. Mayo Clinic warns that humidifiers that are not maintained can make you sick. The EPA advises emptying and refilling tanks daily and cleaning the unit every third day, following the maker's instructions.
          </p>

          <p>
            Wicks complicate cleaning. Most cannot be washed with vinegar or bleach without damaging them, and the makers generally say to replace rather than clean. Clean the tank and base with the method in the manual, rinse thoroughly, and swap the wick on schedule. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-clean-a-humidifier/">cleaning a humidifier</a> walks through vinegar descaling and safe disinfecting.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some newer models build in help. Levoit's Superior Studio has an automatic dry mode that dries the wick when the unit runs out of water, is switched off or finishes descaling, plus a descaling mode, both switchable in the VeSync app. That does not replace cleaning, but drying the wick between uses is one of the most effective ways to slow mold growth.
          </p>

          <p>
            At the end of the season, throw away the old wick, clean and dry every part, and store the unit dry and open. A humidifier put away damp in March is a moldy humidifier in November. Start each winter with a fresh wick.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="placement" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Placement and Humidistats
          </h2>

          <p>
            An evaporative humidifier needs airflow to work. Put it where the fan can draw air freely and push moist air into the room: a few feet from walls and furniture, on a level floor or stand, and out of the path of heating vents that would pull the moisture straight into the return. Keep it away from wood furniture, books and electronics, even though it produces no visible mist.
          </p>

          <p>
            A built-in humidistat turns the unit into something you can set and leave. Vornado says the Evap40's humidistat provides automatic humidity control and shuts the unit off at the chosen level. AIRCARE's MA0800 humidistat adjusts from 25 to 65 percent in 5 percent steps. The Honeywell HEV320 has no humidistat, only two speed settings, so it relies on the natural taper of evaporative output and on you.
          </p>

          <p>
            Built-in sensors sit close to the machine and tend to read high. Put an inexpensive hygrometer across the room and set the humidistat by that reading. Mayo Clinic and the EPA recommend 30 to 50 percent indoors; in very cold weather, lower the target if windows fog, as our <a href="/ideal-indoor-humidity/">ideal indoor humidity</a> guide explains.
          </p>

          <p>
            In a child's room, put the unit out of reach and route the cord where it cannot be pulled. An evaporative unit is a good choice for nurseries because it runs cool, so there is no burn risk, and produces no white dust. Keep it off the floor if a toddler can tip the tank.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="alternatives" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. When an Evaporative Humidifier Is the Wrong Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you need near-silence, buy an ultrasonic model and run it on distilled water. The fan noise of an evaporative unit is the one thing you cannot engineer away, and light sleepers often regret buying one for the bedroom. Our <a href="/best-ultrasonic-humidifier/">best ultrasonic humidifiers</a> guide covers the quiet options.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you want to humidify the whole house, a portable unit may be the wrong tool. A furnace-mounted bypass, fan-powered or steam humidifier uses the duct system to distribute moisture and never needs a tank filled. Our guide to the <a href="/best-whole-house-humidifier/">best whole house humidifiers</a> covers the installed options, which cost more up front but replace several portable units.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your home is already above 50 percent in winter, you do not need a humidifier at all, and adding one can encourage mold and dust mites. Check with a hygrometer before buying. And if you are buying for a child with a cold, any cool-mist type works; the evaporative advantage is about white dust and maintenance, not medical benefit.
          </p>

          <p>
            For everyone else, especially hard-water households who want to use tap water without dust, an evaporative unit sized generously for the room is the most forgiving humidifier you can buy. Order the wicks, set a hygrometer and leave it alone.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the wick schedule</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A scaled, crusty wick evaporates far less water and can start to smell. Makers such as Vornado and Honeywell say to replace wicks every four to eight weeks or 30 to 60 days. Watch for stiffness, odor or falling output, and replace sooner in hard-water areas.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying on the square-footage rating alone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Coverage ratings are best-case figures. AIRCARE's own MA0800 rating drops from 2,600 to 1,700 square feet for average construction. Compare tank size and output too, and size up for open plans, high ceilings or drafty rooms.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not owning a hygrometer</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Evaporative units produce no visible mist, so you cannot see whether they are working. A separate hygrometer across the room tells you whether you are in the 30 to 50 percent range Mayo Clinic and the EPA recommend.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Topping up old water</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Standing water grows bacteria and mold in the tank and base even when the unit releases no droplets. The EPA advises emptying and refilling daily and cleaning every third day. Topping up a tank that has sat for days feeds whatever is already growing.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Storing it damp</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Put away wet in spring, a humidifier is a mold farm by autumn. At the end of the season, discard the wick, clean and dry every part, and store the unit open and dry. Start each winter with a fresh wick.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

