        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best evaporative humidifier?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most rooms we would choose the Vornado Evap40, which Vornado rates for up to 1,000 square feet with a 4-gallon capacity, a built-in humidistat and easy-to-find wicks. For large open spaces the Levoit Superior 6000S has more capacity, and for a quiet bedroom the Levoit Superior Studio Smart has the lowest published noise figures.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do evaporative humidifiers cause white dust?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. White dust comes from minerals in the water being thrown into the air as droplets, which the EPA associates with ultrasonic and impeller humidifiers. An evaporative unit releases only water vapor, and the minerals stay in the wick. The cost is that the wick scales up and needs replacing, sooner with hard water.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often do you replace the wick in an evaporative humidifier?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the model and your water. Vornado says every four to eight weeks, Honeywell says 30 to 60 days for its Filter C, and Levoit says three to six months for the Superior 6000S. Replace sooner if the wick feels hard and crusty, smells or the unit's output drops.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you use tap water in an evaporative humidifier?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, which is one of their main advantages. The wick catches the minerals, so there is no white dust. Hard water makes the wick scale up faster, so expect to replace it more often. Distilled water is not required, but it extends wick life and reduces scale in the tank.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are evaporative humidifiers loud?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They make fan noise, because a fan has to move air across the wick. On low it is usually a gentle hum. Levoit lists its Superior Studio at 19 to 45 decibels and the Superior 6000S at 28 to 45 decibels. Light sleepers who need silence are often better served by an ultrasonic model.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can an evaporative humidifier over-humidify a room?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is much harder than with an ultrasonic unit, because water evaporates more slowly into air that is already humid, so output tapers as the room gets damp. It is not impossible in a small, closed room, so a humidistat or a separate hygrometer is still worth having to hold 30 to 50 percent.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are evaporative humidifiers better than ultrasonic?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They are better if you have hard water, want to use tap water without white dust, or want a unit that is hard to over-run. Ultrasonic models are quieter and have no wick to buy, but need distilled water to avoid dust. Our evaporative vs ultrasonic comparison covers the trade-off in full.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

